 <main id="main"><section class="hero wrap" aria-labelledby="hero-title"><div class="hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="spark"></span> THE ATLAS JOURNAL</p><h1 id="hero-title">What Atlas <br>learned <em>to do.</em></h1><p class="hero-lede">Your world keeps moving.<br>She keeps learning how to carry it.</p><p class="hero-description">The growing record of Atlas: deeper continuity, clearer judgment, and more useful ways to work together. Every entry names its date, event time and timezone — or says what is unavailable. Every claim has a status.</p><a class="hero-link" href="#release-2026-10-19">Explore the latest <span aria-hidden="true">↘</span></a></div><aside class="edition" aria-label="Latest journal edition"><p class="eyebrow">THE LATEST CHAPTER</p><div class="edition-date"><span class="day">19</span><span class="month">OCTOBER<br><b>2026</b></span></div><div class="edition-rule"></div><p class="edition-title">A list that opens,<br>a way to write in.</p><p class="edition-copy">The Abilities page became one list, Anti-Drift Traction got its page, the signup email is being repaired — and the Ubuntu editions are in honest testing.</p><a href="#release-2026-10-19">Read the October 19 record <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><span class="edition-orbit" aria-hidden="true"></span></aside></section>
 <section class="scope wrap" aria-label="Areas covered"><span>ACROSS ATLAS</span><p>Memory <i>·</i> Desktop <i>·</i> Trust <i>·</i> Relationships <i>·</i> Work & life</p></section>
 <section class="journal wrap" id="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title"><aside class="journal-rail"><p class="eyebrow">THE RECORD</p><h2 id="journal-title">Built over time. <br>Kept in view.</h2><nav aria-label="Jump to a date"><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-19"><span>19 Oct 2026</span><span class="latest-tag">LATEST</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-17"><span>17 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-13"><span>13 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-12"><span>12 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-11"><span>11 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-08"><span>08 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-07"><span>07 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-06"><span>06 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-04"><span>04 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-10-03"><span>03 Oct 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-09-27"><span>27 Sep 2026</span></a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-09-17">17 Sep 2026</a><a class="date-link" href="#release-2026-09-16">16 Sep 2026</a></nav><p class="rail-caption">Open a date to explore it.<br>Close it when you’re ready to move on.</p><div class="legend"><h3>Read the status</h3><p><span class="status source">Build progress</span>Implemented in the development build. Release validation continues.</p><p><span class="status validation">In validation</span>Being checked before a release claim.</p><p><span class="status planned">In design</span>A defined direction, not a released feature.</p><p><span class="status archive">From the archive</span>The original public record, with edition clocks distinct from individual event times.</p></div></aside><div class="journal-main"><div class="journal-toolbar"><p><strong>120 entries</strong> · 13 dated records</p><div class="journal-controls" hidden><button type="button" id="expand-all">Expand all</button><button type="button" id="collapse-all">Collapse all</button></div></div><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-19" open><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>19</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">LATEST RECORD · 7 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">A list that opens. A way to write in. Ubuntu in testing.</span><span class="release-subtitle">3 build updates · 4 in validation.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note"><time datetime="2026-10-19">October 19, 2026</time> · Reviewed and added to this journal. Dates identify this journal edition; they do not establish general availability. Status labels apply to each entry.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="world-footer-standard" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">ATLAS SITE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-19">19 Oct 2026 · later website checkpoint</time></div><h3>One aligned footer across the World site.</h3><p>The approved shared footer now covers 41 public HTML pages, including the two legacy pages at Why Atlas and Collision. Navigation, the TITANS signature, hosting credit, legal links and expandable notes follow the same content rail on phones, tablets and desktops. Existing page content, scripts, anchors and disclosures are preserved.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: the earlier 39-page rollout passed independent preservation review, 18 sealed-preview scenes and 12 public browser scenes. This follow-up extends the same component to the two legacy pages. <a href="https://github.com/WeAreTitans/.github/issues/2#issuecomment-6277076487">Earlier deployment record</a>.</p></article><article class="entry" id="ubuntu-free-local-download" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">ATLAS DESKTOP</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-19T21:03:52Z">Verified 19 Oct 2026 · 21:03 UTC</time></div><h3>Ubuntu Free has a public local-beta download.</h3><p>The <a href="/downloads/">Downloads page</a> now offers Atlas Free 0.1.1 for Ubuntu 24.04+ as a local launch beta, with its package link and checksum. Mailbox connection is not available in this build. Lite, Windows and macOS remain unpublished here, and public availability does not complete native, connected-mail or paid-entitlement acceptance.</p><p>The 11:00 UTC Ubuntu entry below is retained as a record of the earlier testing checkpoint. This later entry updates availability without changing that history.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: the public Downloads page and its published Free checksum, <code>ad9575e7f3ef1eb63f46093f0106a07a27798bfb859a88d5651359775dd664a7</code>, read at this checkpoint. No new installer or mailbox test is claimed by this journal update.</p></article><article class="entry" id="abilities-one-list" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">ATLAS SITE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-19T10:43:55Z">19 Oct 2026 · 10:43:55 UTC</time></div><h3>The Abilities page became one list.</h3><p>The six sections that followed the “Where she stands” table are now rows 08 to 13 of the same table, each with its status chips and a “+” that opens the full detail; the closing promise is 14. Rows open in place, one at a time, and an in-page link such as “Trust” opens its row and takes you to it.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: headless-browser run of the live page at desktop and phone widths, 42 link clicks per width, no errors; page text compared against the version before the change · <code>785755f6c12666b0…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="adt-page" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">TRUST &amp; EXECUTION</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-19T10:59:22Z">19 Oct 2026 · 10:59:22 UTC</time></div><h3>Titan.Anti-Drift Traction has its own page.</h3><p>It is a key muscle at the heart of Titan.Mnemos: the admission layer being engineered to keep Atlas, and every agent working under it, inside the work you actually authorized. The new page on Tech gives an everyday example and says plainly that it is under qualification — a design under test, not a shipped guarantee.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: the page and its links from the Tech index and from Titan.Mnemos, read live on 19 Oct 2026.</p></article><article class="entry" id="about-email-us" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">ATLAS SITE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-19T10:07:01Z">19 Oct 2026 · 10:07:01 UTC</time></div><h3>Eleven Titan sites now have an About page and an Email us form.</h3><p>A message sent through the form is saved privately and is not published. The sender gets a receipt number; the receipt confirms delivery, not a response time. The Atlas sites went first, and the rest followed later the same morning.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: each of the eight newest forms accepted a clearly labelled test message and returned a receipt, 19 Oct 2026.</p></article><article class="entry" id="signup-confirmation" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">ATLAS SITE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-19">19 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The signup confirmation email was repaired. Inbox placement is not proven yet.</h3><p>A test registration was accepted, but its confirmation email did not arrive. The sender was changed to an address whose published records authorize the mail server, and a new test message was accepted by Gmail — into the Spam folder. The public record for the mail signature (DKIM) has since been published for titanatlas.live, and a fresh test message signed with it was accepted by Gmail. Whether it reached the inbox rather than Spam is still unproven.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: the domain’s published SPF, DMARC and DKIM records, read 19 Oct 2026 at 12:05 UTC; delivery results from two earlier test messages.</p></article><article class="entry" id="ubuntu-pair-testing" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">ATLAS DESKTOP</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-19T11:00:00Z">19 Oct 2026 · 11:00 UTC</time></div><h3>Atlas Free and Lite for Ubuntu are in testing, not released.</h3><p>In an offline check on one test profile, the two editions share no files and keep separate data folders, and starting Lite left Free’s saved data byte-identical. Still to prove: connecting a real mailbox (Google’s registration file is not yet in either build), the account grant that unlocks Lite, upgrade and rollback, and signing. Lite stays locked until that grant exists. Nothing here is released.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: independent combination check on a Titan test machine, 19 Oct 2026 · 11:00 to 11:04 UTC · packages <code>087d590f…</code> (Free) and <code>06231cb9…</code> (Lite).</p></article></div></div></details><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-17"><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>17</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">LATEST RECORD · 12 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">Installers exist. The bracket is honest. The family grew.</span><span class="release-subtitle">9 build updates · 2 in validation · 1 in design.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note"><time datetime="2026-10-17">October 17, 2026</time> · Reviewed and added to this journal. Dates identify this journal edition; they do not establish general availability. Status labels apply to each entry.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="win-editions-r2" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN WINDOWS</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Atlas Free and Lite became real installers.</h3><p>The first distinct Free and Lite Windows installers were produced, installed and edition-verified, then rebuilt on the corrected source — 424 native court checks green, install-over-install upgrade proven. Public admission still waits on signing and entitlement gates.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: WIN-PUBLIC-EDITIONS-R1/R2 receipts · <code>2fbf5e66507406e5…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="launch-packet-v7" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN WINDOWS</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The launch packet consolidated into one verified archive.</h3><p>Seven revisions of the consolidated launch packet were produced and hash-verified across the day — 485 payloads covering candidates, reviews, Charlie source, the Quark design reconciliation and this record. Installer binaries stay outside the packet until signing clears.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: ATLAS_WINDOWS_LAUNCH_PACKET_20261017 v7 · <code>f2e6c0cfe8f799ef…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="orion-battery-v2" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">TITAN MODEL RESEARCH</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>A 30-model bracket is crowning Atlas's local brains.</h3><p>The Orion World Cup runs every candidate through the same 25-task card with sealed, hash-chained receipts. After a contaminated first pass was honestly tombstoned, the clean v2 run crowned its first champion — <code>ministral-3:3b</code> took the feather class at 0.7191/0.6728. Remaining classes are still fighting; no champion is claimed without a sealed ledger.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: battery-feather-20261017-v2 ledger · <code>f7da760a9a13e8b2…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="challenger-bouts" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">TITAN MODEL RESEARCH</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Fresh challengers get a fair fight, losers retire.</h3><p>New releases — MiniCPM5-2B, Gemma 4 (e4b / 26b-a4b / 31b) and Granite 4.2 — were pulled into the store to face each class champion in staggered bouts. A defeated model's manifest and exclusive blobs are removed only after a refcount check proves nothing else references them; <code>gemma3:4b</code> was the first to fall and retire.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: run-bouts.sh + delete-loser.py · <code>0da66eca2de8870a…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="fleet-updates" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN FLEET</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Every machine now updates itself — and tells Atlas.</h3><p>Daily self-update passes run on a cron on Orion and a scheduled task on Thor. Each node writes a status record the Atlas Fleet Updates module renders — successes, failures and sudo-blocked steps included, never hidden.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: titan-fleet-update.sh/.ps1 + fleet module · <code>a1ff13fc699d26c9…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="ollama-0404" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN FLEET</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The model runtime jumped four generations.</h3><p>Orion's system Ollama went 0.21.2 → 0.40.4, the user binary to 0.40.6 bytes, unlocking the qwen35-architecture models that silently failed before. The battery endpoint restarted clean — all 30 models serving.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: Orion fleet update evidence · <code>f7da760a9a13e8b2…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="sadie-android" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN FAMILY</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Sadie got a phone body.</h3><p>The Sadie Android shell was scaffolded in Kotlin — a faithful 1:1 port of the sealed reference engine, hash-chained receipts, the shrine doctrine built into the face: one calm scene, observances not check-ins, no location permission, no microphone, no hidden tracking. Eleven reference cases and four commerce cases are specified; they run once a JDK/SDK is installed — it is honestly uncompiled until then.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: sadie-native/apps/android · <code>5574a59ff1395c8f…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="commerce-lane" data-status="planned"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status planned">In design</span><span class="technology">TITAN FAMILY</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Family shopping links, disclosed by construction.</h3><p>An affiliate registry carries Amazon and other merchant links with the required disclosure bound into each link object — a commerce surface cannot render without it. The tag stays a clearly-marked inactive placeholder until a real Associates ID is issued. Amazon's child-directed exclusion applies to Quark.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: AffiliateRegistry.kt + acceptance tests · <code>5574a59ff1395c8f…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="quark-coppa" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN TRUST</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Quark's duties to children are written into the record.</h3><p>The public privacy notice gained a full COPPA section: verifiable guardian consent before any under-13 data exists, absolute guardian review and deletion, minimum collection, and a hard rule that no advertising, affiliate link or sponsored content ever reaches a child. The capability catalog carries the same commitments as a machine-readable entry.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: privacy/index.html + capability catalog · <code>44decbdf6d2a2800…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="charlie-builders" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN LOCAL INTELLIGENCE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>One coordinator, many builders — proven in the court.</h3><p>The candidate Charlie coordinator→builders pattern passed its synthetic court: digest-pinned model calls, retained builder results, signed terminal events and crash-recovery repair — 26 suite checks plus the continuation layer's 58. This proves the pattern; installed takeover through the live route remains its own gate.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: charlie-join-r1 candidate evidence · <code>0da66eca2de8870a…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="disk-reclaim" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN FLEET</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>36 GB of dead weight came off Orion.</h3><p>Seven stale Rust build trees' target directories plus index caches were deleted — pure artifacts, no evidence touched. Free space went 359 → 395 GB. The real model cleanup still waits behind the champions, as it should.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: Orion disk audit 2026-10-17 · <code>f7da760a9a13e8b2…</code></p></article><article class="entry" id="wordmark-zero-breaks" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">TITAN BRAND</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-17">17 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The face finally obeys its own law — everywhere.</h3><p>A full sweep of the public site found 14 wordmark-law breaks: a teal dot and red ATLAS instead of the mandated saffron/blue/white, a heavy wrong-face hero, a non-canonical SVG, and family names in public copy. All repaired to canon — the checker now reports zero breaks.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: wordmark law sweep 2026-10-17 · <code>44decbdf6d2a2800…</code></p></article></div></div></details><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-13"><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>13</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">THE ROLLING RECORD · 1 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">BlackVault gets a front door in Settings.</span><span class="release-subtitle">1 build update.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note"><time datetime="2026-10-13">October 13, 2026</time> · Rolled in from the Atlas live-updates feed (revision 38). Dates identify the listing day; status labels apply to each entry.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="blackvault-settings-entrance" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">PRIVACY</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-13T01:33:34Z">13 Oct 2026 · 01:33 UTC</time></div><h3>A Settings entrance for BlackVault</h3><p>BlackVault, the encrypted vault that stays on your computer, now has a permanent entry in Settings as well as in setup. In narrower windows the same card stays on Home so the player and tabs keep their room.</p></article></div></div></details><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-12"><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>12</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">THE ROLLING RECORD · 7 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">Converge, a learning router, and a quieter Home.</span><span class="release-subtitle">5 build updates · 1 in validation · 1 in design.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note"><time datetime="2026-10-12">October 12, 2026</time> · Rolled in from the Atlas live-updates feed (revision 38). Dates identify the listing day; status labels apply to each entry.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="titan-converge" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Building now</span><span class="technology">FAMILY</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-12T00:21:16Z">12 Oct 2026 · 00:21 UTC</time></div><h3>Titan Converge</h3><p>Live progress from the family&#x27;s working agents, gathered in one place and passed back to Atlas, Nessa and Savant, so you can see in real time how far the work has moved.</p></article><article class="entry" id="fusion-adapt-learning-router" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">INTELLIGENCE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-12T00:21:26Z">12 Oct 2026 · 00:21 UTC</time></div><h3>Fusion Adapt: the learning router</h3><p>Sends each request to the cheapest route that has proven itself, counts a saving only once and only after two independent checks agree, and refuses bad numbers before they reach its log. Version 1.2: 294 tests pass on our machine; not yet wired into Atlas.</p></article><article class="entry" id="titan-black-vault" data-status="planned"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status planned">In design</span><span class="technology">INTELLIGENCE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-12T01:43:31Z">12 Oct 2026 · 01:43 UTC</time></div><h3>Titan Black Vault</h3><p>Encrypted storage for the family&#x27;s engineering and memories, with the master keys kept outside Atlas, separate compartments for each kind of data, and every worker session in its own sandbox.</p></article><article class="entry" id="player-album-art" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">HOME</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-12T13:02:59Z">12 Oct 2026 · 13:02 UTC</time></div><h3>Album art in the player</h3><p>The cover of the song that is playing now shows in the player on the left. One small picture is fetched from the music service and kept in memory only.</p></article><article class="entry" id="home-weather-five-days" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">HOME</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-12T13:03:04Z">12 Oct 2026 · 13:03 UTC</time></div><h3>Weather on Home: today and four days, next three hours</h3><p>The Home weather card shows today and the next four days, with a small bubble beside it for the next three hours. Wide windows get a larger card and tiles.</p></article><article class="entry" id="afterglow-cloud-chamber" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">AFTERGLOW</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-12T15:25:11Z">12 Oct 2026 · 15:25 UTC</time></div><h3>AfterGlow: a quiet particle chamber</h3><p>While music plays, the AfterGlow stage draws fine, curved particle tracks, small decays and tiny collisions, in place of the big equalizer bars. Each song gets its own pattern. It pauses when the music pauses and stands still if your system asks for reduced motion.</p></article><article class="entry" id="clipboard-history" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">PRIVACY</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-12T15:25:16Z">12 Oct 2026 · 15:25 UTC</time></div><h3>Clipboard history, kept in memory only</h3><p>An optional list of what you copied, held in memory only: never written to disk, gone after a day or when Atlas closes, and not kept while the vault is open. Off until you turn it on.</p></article></div></div></details><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-11"><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>11</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">THE ROLLING RECORD · 15 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">Five languages, a live build board, and the intelligence bench.</span><span class="release-subtitle">12 build updates · 2 in validation · 1 in design.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note"><time datetime="2026-10-11">October 11, 2026</time> · Rolled in from the Atlas live-updates feed (revision 38). Dates identify the listing day; status labels apply to each entry.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="atlas-five-languages" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">LANGUAGE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:35:33Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:35 UTC</time></div><h3>Atlas speaks five languages</h3><p>English, Chinese, Hindi, Spanish and Arabic, switched from the left menu, with right-to-left layout for Arabic. Native-speaker review continues.</p></article><article class="entry" id="atlas-plain-english" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">DESKTOP</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:35:33Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:35 UTC</time></div><h3>Every screen in plain English</h3><p>About 900 lines across Atlas rewritten in plain words, from the morning greeting to the approval cards.</p></article><article class="entry" id="realm-inner-world" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">REALM</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:35:34Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:35 UTC</time></div><h3>Realm: the Digital Inner World</h3><p>Seven fixed places for the family around Atlas&#x27;s globe, with a read-only inspector. Passed 1,748 tests; held back from the desktop until the centre globe draws at full size in the real app.</p></article><article class="entry" id="adapt-probability-court" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">INTELLIGENCE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:35:34Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:35 UTC</time></div><h3>ADAPT: scored probabilities</h3><p>The scored-forecast court runs on Atlas&#x27;s real turns: 43 so far, 79 percent answered in time. Every forecast is written down first, then scored. No model beats the plain base rate yet; more turns will tell.</p></article><article class="entry" id="atlas-live-board" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Live</span><span class="technology">WEB</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:35:34Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:35 UTC</time></div><h3>This live board</h3><p>Live on this site: the board updates the moment a module starts, changes stage or ships, and greets whatever is new since your last visit.</p></article><article class="entry" id="adapt-research-domains" data-status="planned"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status planned">In design</span><span class="technology">RESEARCH</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:35:34Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:35 UTC</time></div><h3>ADAPT for Steward, Bunkers and Maritime research</h3><p>The same scored-forecast court for money, bunker-fuel and maritime research. In design: nothing is sold, advised or traded from it.</p></article><article class="entry" id="network-skylines" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">NETWORK</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:48:18Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:48 UTC</time></div><h3>Network Skylines</h3><p>TitanPulse and Skylines merged into one view above Realm: every Titan machine as a tower, with live readings only where they are measured.</p></article><article class="entry" id="afterglow-in-atlas" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">MUSIC</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:48:23Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:48 UTC</time></div><h3>AfterGlow inside Atlas</h3><p>A small player with a moving equaliser in the left pane, and a full AfterGlow view. Now wired to the desktop&#x27;s own music players (Spotify included): play, pause, next and previous, read on this machine only.</p></article><article class="entry" id="fusion-value-meter" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">INTELLIGENCE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:48:27Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:48 UTC</time></div><h3>Fusion value, in real time</h3><p>What Fusion and Fusion Adapt save the owner, counted from real routing receipts. Estimates are labelled as estimates until measured runs exist.</p></article><article class="entry" id="cleo-parity" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">EVENT HORIZON</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T18:48:36Z">11 Oct 2026 · 18:48 UTC</time></div><h3>Cleo catches up</h3><p>Cleo gets the same orange menu, live weather with the next three hours, Steward and Guardian.</p></article><article class="entry" id="titan-four-diamonds" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Live</span><span class="technology">BRAND</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T19:11:49Z">11 Oct 2026 · 19:11 UTC</time></div><h3>The four Titan diamonds</h3><p>White on top, saffron behind, teal forward, red below: soft corners, softly blurred. Now the tab icon on WeAreTitans.</p></article><article class="entry" id="probability-math-excavation" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Building now</span><span class="technology">INTELLIGENCE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T19:11:54Z">11 Oct 2026 · 19:11 UTC</time></div><h3>Our own probability math, recovered</h3><p>The quantum-style field kernel, the potential-movement engine and the assurance stamp, brought together and re-tested for ADAPT.</p></article><article class="entry" id="fusion-adapt-evidence-bridge" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">INTELLIGENCE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T22:44:14Z">11 Oct 2026 · 22:44 UTC</time></div><h3>Fusion ADAPT evidence contracts</h3><p>Offline adaptive routing and paired outcome accounting pass 159 source checks; the progress contract passes 45. Development source is added. Live model benchmarks, trusted receipt admission and runtime activation remain pending.</p></article><article class="entry" id="collision-feed-view" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Live</span><span class="technology">WEB</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T22:44:24Z">11 Oct 2026 · 22:44 UTC</time></div><h3>Collision view of the live build board</h3><p>Live on this site. The separate Collision view reads the existing public build board, refreshes every 30 seconds and preserves development history. Live model benchmarks remain pending.</p></article><article class="entry" id="fusion-flux-offline-planning" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">INTELLIGENCE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-11T23:09:30Z">11 Oct 2026 · 23:09 UTC</time></div><h3>Fusion and Flux offline planning</h3><p>Offline planning and cache checks pass with scripted fixtures. Real model routing, automatic learning and live benchmarks remain pending.</p></article></div></div></details><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-08"><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>8</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">LATEST RECORD · 5 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">A commercial spine. A face that moves.</span><span class="release-subtitle">4 build updates · 1 in design.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note"><time datetime="release-2026-10-08">October 8, 2026</time> · Reviewed and added to this journal. Dates identify this journal edition; they do not establish general availability. Status labels apply to each entry.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="commercial-law" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">COMMERCIAL LAW</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-08">8 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The price on the page is now the law.</h3><p>Pricing, Terms, and Privacy were rewritten to carry the frozen launch terms: SynOct LLC as seller of record, Dark.Matter at $100/month, Event.Horizon at $200/month with Nessa included, the 30-day free trial, the 72-hour grace period, and the promise that billing problems never delete your local Atlas.</p></article><article class="entry" id="local-gateway" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">PUBLIC AGENTS</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-08">8 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The public agents are being moved to their own house.</h3><p>A local gateway was installed on the Atlas host: same contract, no external API keys, fail-closed by design. It opens when a qualified local model is bound to it — until then every public agent stays honestly degraded.</p></article><article class="entry" id="face-alive" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">ATLAS ALIVE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-08">8 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The face moves like something alive.</h3><p>The hero sphere had every state built — rest, attending, working, deep — and nothing ever called them. Now she turns as a globe of particles, fires a saffron shockwave when she shifts to work, spins a four-color rim, and answers a visitor’s touch. Roughly four times faster at rest, and still silent under reduced-motion.</p></article><article class="entry" id="console-clean" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">PUBLIC ESTATE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-08">8 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The browser console is clean for the first time.</h3><p>The site’s strict security policy had been silently blocking page behavior — an inline script that never ran and an inline style that never applied. Both were moved where the policy allows them; the public page now loads without a single console violation.</p></article><article class="entry" id="board-hold" data-status="planned"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status planned">In design</span><span class="technology">LAUNCH</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-08">8 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The launch board still says HOLD — and that is the point.</h3><p>Payments and the People lane are not yet proven, so the board does not say green. Every leg of launch acceptance — web, email, join, identity, claims, people — must carry evidence before it flips.</p></article></div></div></details><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-07"><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>7</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">LATEST RECORD · 5 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">The estate becomes honest, page by page.</span><span class="release-subtitle">4 build updates · 1 in validation.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note"><time datetime="release-2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time> · Reviewed and added to this journal. Dates identify this journal edition; they do not establish general availability. Status labels apply to each entry.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="family-ledger" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">ENTITY TRUTH</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-07">7 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The family ledger now says what launched.</h3><p>TitanArray’s public record lists its launched sites — including this one — with canonical URLs, and the role split is stated plainly: SynOct carries the research, TitanArray is the publisher, WeAreTitans is the house.</p></article><article class="entry" id="indexing-open" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">PUBLIC ESTATE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-07">7 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Search engines may now read the public pages.</h3><p>The marketing estate had an empty robots policy and noindex tags left over from before launch preparation. The gate was opened on public pages while private and prototype surfaces stay excluded.</p></article><article class="entry" id="acquisition-door" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">ACQUISITION</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-07">7 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The door to Atlas actually opens now.</h3><p>Get Atlas now leads somewhere real: a join route, a durable intake, and the customer site carrying the same door — instead of a link to nowhere. Requests are labeled launch intake, honestly, not a support case system that does not exist yet.</p></article><article class="entry" id="communities-honest" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">COMMUNITIES</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-07">7 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Communities tells the truth about itself.</h3><p>The communities page was re-marked as a workbench preview — what is built versus what is open — with a real door to the live forums instead of a quiet placeholder pretending to be a product.</p></article><article class="entry" id="ollie-honest" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">PUBLIC AGENTS</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-07">7 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Ollie says what he can actually do.</h3><p>The public agent now answers from the curated corpus and says so plainly instead of faking a live brain. His gateway lane is installed but fail-closed until a qualified model is bound — degraded honestly, not decorated.</p></article></div></div></details><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-06"><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>6</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">LATEST RECORD · 4 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">The audit that started the fixes.</span><span class="release-subtitle">4 build updates.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note"><time datetime="release-2026-10-06">October 6, 2026</time> · Reviewed and added to this journal. Dates identify this journal edition; they do not establish general availability. Status labels apply to each entry.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="estate-audit" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">PUBLIC ESTATE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-06">6 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>Every public page got read before launch.</h3><p>The full public estate — Atlas, WeAreTitans, TitanArray, and SynOct — was crawled page by page ahead of launch. Every contradiction, stale claim, and broken route found was entered into the fix ledger.</p></article><article class="entry" id="one-story" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">PUBLIC ESTATE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-06">6 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>One Atlas. One story.</h3><p>The public pages used to describe the Titan machine fleet two different ways on the same page — "13 machines" and "14 machines" sentences apart. The count was corrected everywhere it appeared.</p></article><article class="entry" id="honest-chrome" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">PUBLIC ESTATE</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-06">6 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The header stopped predicting the future.</h3><p>A hardcoded line in the page chrome claimed a production status that had not been measured that day. The verification stamp is now what it is — a timestamp — and the live status is computed at read time, not printed in advance.</p></article><article class="entry" id="wordmark-law" data-status="source"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status source">Build progress</span><span class="technology">VISUAL LAW</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-06">6 Oct 2026 · event time and timezone unavailable</time></div><h3>The wordmark follows one law.</h3><p>TITAN in white, the dot in saffron, ATLAS in red — the same law now applies across the public wordmark files instead of drifting between pages.</p></article></div></div></details><details class="release" id="release-2026-10-04"><summary><span class="date-tile"><b>4</b><span>OCT <span class="year">2026</span></span></span><span class="release-heading"><span class="release-kicker">LATEST RECORD · 4 ENTRIES</span><span class="release-title">Measured Desktop progress. Production proof still waits.</span><span class="release-subtitle">Every event has its own verified UTC timestamp and scope.</span></span><span class="disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="release-body"><div class="release-note">Development evidence, checked against the frozen source epoch. Later GPU work is in progress; these earlier results do not cover it. OS production remains HOLD.</div><div class="entries"><article class="entry" id="canonical-release-boundary-20261004" data-status="validation"><div class="entry-meta"><span class="status validation">In validation</span><span class="technology">RELEASE STATUS</span><time class="entry-time" datetime="2026-10-04T10:56:07Z">4 Oct 2026 · 10:56:07 UTC</time></div><h3>Current release authority stays unavailable.</h3><p>A source review confirmed that the current promotion observer and acceptance CLI keep release and candidate-label authority disabled. The release-receipt shape remains a reserved draft. Desktop engineering progress and ongoing GPU implementation do not change the production OS HOLD.</p><p class="entry-evidence">Evidence: Canonical source review during website correction · <code>2a4ced6226417470…</code></p></article>
